# Code owners for release-critical surface.
#
# How this works: CODEOWNERS is LAST-MATCH-WINS, and paths that match no rule
# have NO owner. Combined with "Require review from Code Owners" on main, that
# means:
#
#   - A PR touching only unowned paths (e.g. src/, README.md) needs just the
#     normal 1 approval -- any maintainer can review and merge it.
#   - A PR touching an owned path additionally requires @MrMarble's approval.
#
# Nobody else is listed on purpose. Listing a second owner for "the rest" would
# make THEIR review mandatory on ordinary PRs, including PRs authored by
# @MrMarble -- the opposite of the intent.
#
# The threat model: malicious code may reach main, but a tagged release cannot
# be cut without @MrMarble approving. Every path below is either (a) a file
# release-please rewrites on each release PR, so owning it gates releases, or
# (b) build/CI surface that could inject into the published artifact or
# redirect the update channel WITHOUT appearing in a release PR diff.
#
# NOTE: reviewing the release PR diff alone is NOT sufficient -- it is three
# files of version bumps. Review the accumulated main diff since the previous
# tag; the release PR body lists every included commit.

# CI, workflows, dependabot -- and this file itself, so ownership rules cannot
# be edited without @MrMarble. publish.yml builds and uploads the artifact and
# holds RELEASE_PLEASE_TOKEN.
/.github/                       @MrMarble

# Written by release-please on every release PR: these two are the gate.
# Never hand-edited, so they fire on release PRs and essentially nothing else.
/CHANGELOG.md                   @MrMarble
/.release-please-manifest.json  @MrMarble

# Controls HOW releases are cut: draft, force-tag-creation, changelog-sections,
# title patterns. Flipping "draft" or un-hiding a section changes release
# behaviour without touching any release PR.
/release-please-config.json     @MrMarble

# Build surface that lands in the published .user.js.
# package.json carries the version AND `downloadURL`, which rollup injects as
# the userscript's @downloadURL -- changing it hijacks auto-update for every
# existing user. It also gates `scripts`, which run in CI.
/package.json                   @MrMarble
/pnpm-lock.yaml                 @MrMarble
/rollup.config.mjs              @MrMarble
/babel.config.cjs               @MrMarble
/src/meta.js                    @MrMarble
